#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119570 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34272b7-a7ad-5a58-974e-54cca957d06e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "01f32313-178c-5626-91af-110907202416" :question/math-academy-id "e-4936" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(9,-2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}=3\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+7\\mathbf{k}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 9,-2,3 \\rangle$ and the normal vector of the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 3,-4,7 \\rangle.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =⟨9,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =56 \\\\ 3x-4y+7z & =56 \\\\ 3x-4y+7z-56 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119571 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line"}] :question/id #uuid "d92eb242-104f-55cd-82dc-7620e2f4e636" :question/math-academy-id "e-4937" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-1,4,8)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\dfrac{x+3}{-5}=\\dfrac{y-1}{3}=\\dfrac{z+2}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -1,4,8 \\rangle.$ From the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =⟨-1,4,8⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =33 \\\\ -5x+3y+2z & =33 \\\\ -5x+3y+2z-33 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119572 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "aa7c3400-9bc0-5f3a-84e5-f3cc2bb87f5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4938" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $2x-y+z+13=0$ of the plane $\\Pi,$ find an equation of $\\Pi$ in the form $(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot\\mathbf{n}=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the coefficients in equation of the plane, we get that the normal vector of $\\Pi$ is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 2,-1,1 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\Pi$ for $z\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y+z+13 & =0 \\\\ z & =-2x+y-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0,$ we get $z=-13.$ So, the point $P(0,0,-13)$ lies on $\\Pi.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 0,0,-13 \\rangle.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-13⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,-1,1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119573 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0efe5b11-2c87-58e9-bf7c-e1eecb07adf2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "8a9e1070-0ca0-5f24-ac89-999c9271a91b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4939" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,-6)$ and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{v_1}=\\langle -3, 5, 2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}=\\langle 1, 3, -2 \\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,-6 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-16i-4j-14k \\\\  & =⟨-16,-4,-14⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =⟨-3,2,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =124\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =124 \\\\ -16x-4y-14z & =124 \\\\ -16x-4y-14z-124 & =0 \\\\ 8x+2y+7z+62 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317151 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34272b7-a7ad-5a58-974e-54cca957d06e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74e40a26-493d-5b63-b2a7-b0c0857a63c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-3y-z+13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317153} {:answer/id #uuid "96600223-6ac2-5aae-af5f-d339a3bf95ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+3y-z-11=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317154} {:answer/id #uuid "8fad3f9f-dd27-58db-96a2-20d9a9edd43f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+4y-z-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317155} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd52b89-44ba-5ac6-aa3e-ee8390f035fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-4y-z+9=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317156} {:answer/id #uuid "83c33c72-a071-596b-90b3-cb3ce07d269b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3y-4z-17=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fad3f9f-dd27-58db-96a2-20d9a9edd43f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+4y-z-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317155} :answer-field/id #uuid "39431669-f8b7-5624-822e-3855a0a51429" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "03ce23ca-0088-57fb-b4ca-21f59a96ca63" :question/math-academy-id "q-127967" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-2,3,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=3i+4j-k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-2,3,-1⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨3,4,-1⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,4,-1⟩ & =⟨-2,3,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨3,4,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,4,-1⟩ & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,4,-1⟩ & =7 \\\\ 3x+4y-z & =7 \\\\ 3x+4y-z-7 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34272b7-a7ad-5a58-974e-54cca957d06e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd320510-1075-5e34-8eeb-c578ca849a56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+y-5z+26=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317188} {:answer/id #uuid "6489f1d6-5bad-54b1-8041-e0b0f9e9e222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y-5z+24=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317189} {:answer/id #uuid "7d72cb60-ea74-5c35-8b5a-0618e41e1504" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y+5z+26=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317190} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b80d53-2e9a-538d-9b63-efb1fb02a239" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y-5z-26=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317191} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7a3741-4493-561e-8fef-e14d90b81345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y-5z+26=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c7a3741-4493-561e-8fef-e14d90b81345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y-5z+26=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317192} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b3cc707-a5f8-5ce6-958c-76852cfc90f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc6b7572-6525-5f0a-acdf-da49eecac498" :question/math-academy-id "q-330498" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,-2,4)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨0,3,-5⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨1,-2,4⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨0,3,-5⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ & =⟨1,-2,4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ & =-26 \\\\ 3y-5z & =-26 \\\\ 3y-5z+26 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
